- How would you test for starvation under mixed-key traffic?
- How would you adapt this for distributed workers across nodes?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates a candidate's practical understanding of per-source ordering with shared throughput, backpressure mechanisms, and graceful degradation in a realistic chat notification system.

## Key Concepts

- **Per-room FIFO buffers**: Ensure events within each room maintain insertion order
- **Global bounded worker queue**: Limit total memory usage while enabling cross-room parallelism
- **Backpressure thresholds**: Prevent producers from overwhelming the system
- **Degradation via event coalescing**: Reduce load by merging rapid state changes (typing indicators) into single events
- **Graceful shutdown**: Drain in-flight work before terminating without data loss

## Code Example

```javascript
class RoomBuffer {
  constructor(maxBacklog = 1000) {
    this.queues = new Map();
    this.maxBacklog = maxBacklog;
  }

  enqueue(room, event) {
    if (!this.queues.has(room)) {
      this.queues.set(room, []);
    }
    const queue = this.queues.get(room);
    if (queue.length >= this.maxBacklog) {
      if (event.type === 'typing') {
        return this.coalesceTyping(room, event);
      }
      return false; // backpressure signal
    }
    queue.push({ ...event, seq: this.getNextSeq(room) });
    return true;
  }

  coalesceTyping(room, event) {
    const queue = this.queues.get(room);
    const last = queue[queue.length - 1];
    if (last?.type === 'typing') {
      last.count = (last.count || 1) + 1;
      return true;
    }
    queue.push({ ...event, count: 1 });
    return true;
  }
}
```

This implementation provides per-room ordering via sequence numbers, backpressure via bounded queue depth, and automatic coalescing of typing events during high load.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design correct, robust concurrency under backpressure with priority aging and graceful shutdown.

## Key Concepts
- Bounded queues and backpressure
- Priority-aware scheduling
- Fairness via aging
- Graceful shutdown and cancellation

## Code Example
```java
import java.util.concurrent.*;

class WorkItem { final int priority; final Runnable task; WorkItem(int p, Runnable t){priority=p;this.task=t;} void run(){task.run();} }

public class MultiPriorityScheduler {
  private final BlockingQueue<WorkItem>[] queues = new BlockingQueue[3];
  private volatile boolean running = true;

  @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
  public MultiPriorityScheduler(int cap){
    for(int i=0;i<3;i++) queues[i] = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(cap);
  }

  public boolean submit(WorkItem w, long timeout, TimeUnit unit) throws InterruptedException {
    return queues[w.priority].offer(w, timeout, unit);
  }

  public void start(int workerCount){
    for(int i=0;i<workerCount;i++){
      new Thread(() -> {
        while(running){
          WorkItem w=null;
          for(int p=0;p<3;p++){
            w = queues[p].poll(50, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);
            if (w != null){ w.run(); break; }
          }
        }
      }).start();
    }
  }

  public void stop(){ running = false; }
}
```

